================================================================================
TEST T44979 -- ACL: Named Hardware on port - Proto
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 (times NZ, box local; device clocks set to box local time by
          the group's setup)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-8646, "ACL: Named Hardware on port - Proto", baseline plus 8 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, netbooted),
                 boot config flash:/default.cfg
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (AT-x960-28YSQ, console u3)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: yes,
          for STEP 7)

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL matching an IP protocol number, bound to a port
         with `access-group`, denies exactly the matching flow and nothing else.
      protocol-number match on a port .. denied     (proto 1, 100% loss from .65)
      non-matching source ............. permitted  (0% loss from .66, same path)
      protocol selectivity ............ yes        (a proto 6 rule leaves ICMP alone)
      first match wins ................ yes        (seq 1 permit beats seq 4 deny)
      edit of a list while bound ...... applies    (entry appears and takes effect)
      non-IP (ARP) traffic ............ unaffected (re-resolved while ICMP denied)
      same ACL via service-policy ..... works      (access-group proven gone first)
      persistence across a reload ..... holds      (config and enforcement survived)
      removal ......................... clean      (classifier back to baseline)
CAVEAT:  The traffic used throughout is ICMP echo, sent from tb277 eth4 into
         port1.0.48. `access-group` is an ingress filter, so only that direction was
         filtered; the reply direction was not tested. The protocol 6 rule was
         exercised only as the negative control for ICMP -- no TCP flow was generated,
         so this run does not establish that a proto 6 rule denies TCP. Protocol
         numbers other than 1 and 6 were not tried.

