STEP 0 -- baseline, nothing configured                                    done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
                                            <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  Loop Detection      2
  ACL                 0
  Total               2 / 1024 (0.20%)      <-- TCAM baseline
host# ping .70 from .65 -> 0% loss ; from .66 -> 0% loss
  Both sources reach the far side before any ACL exists.

STEP 1 -- "match an IP protocol number in a NAMED hardware ACL on a port"  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44979
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44979
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
                                            <-- `proto 1` CANONICALISED to `icmp` by the
                                                CLI, both stored and displayed that way
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
   access-group t44979
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss      PERMITTED
  => STEP 1 PASS: a named hardware ACL matching an IP protocol number, bound to a port
     with `access-group`, denies the matching flow and leaves an identical flow from
     another source on the same port untouched.

STEP 2 -- a named list CAN be edited while it is bound to an interface    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979
  % This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied
    to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode by entering "exit"
    or "end" command.
                                            <-- ADVISORY, not a refusal: the prompt
                                                advanced and the edit was accepted
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 permit proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44979
      1 permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      4 deny   icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
                                            <-- the new entry IS in the bound list
  => STEP 2 PASS: an entry added to a named hardware ACL while that ACL was attached to
     port1.0.48 was accepted and appears in the list. The `%` line the switch printed is
     an advisory about when the change is applied, not a rejection; STEP 3 shows the
     edit also took effect in the dataplane.

STEP 3 -- "first match applies"                                           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
With `1 permit` ahead of `4 deny`, both naming the identical flow:
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss        PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 1
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED again
  => STEP 3 PASS: where two entries match the same traffic with opposite actions, the
     lower sequence number wins; removing it restores the later rule. Evaluation is
     first-match. This is also the dataplane proof that the STEP 2 edit applied.

STEP 4 -- "non-matching traffic passes" -- protocol SELECTIVITY           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a second list is written for protocol 6 and bound in place of the first, on
  the same port with the same source and destination as STEP 1. STEP 1 and STEP 4 are
  each other's controls: only the protocol number differs.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44979b
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny proto 6 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44979b
      4 deny tcp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32    <-- proto 6 canonicalised to `tcp`
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44979 ; access-group t44979b
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss        ICMP NOT denied
  => STEP 4 PASS: a rule matching protocol 6 leaves ICMP alone, on the same port, same
     source and same destination where protocol 1 denied it in STEP 1. The protocol
     field genuinely selects rather than matching everything.

STEP 5 -- non-IP traffic unaffected                                       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
host# ip neigh flush dev eth4 ; ping .70 from .65 (denied)
host# ip neigh show dev eth4
  10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE
                                            <-- ARP crossed the path and was relearned
  => STEP 5 PASS: ARP re-resolved across the path while ICMP from the same host was
     denied. An IP hardware ACL does not filter non-IP frames.

STEP 6 -- "repeat via service policy"                                     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: an interface ACL is matched BEFORE any QoS policy on the same port, so the
  access-group must be removed and PROVEN gone first; otherwise the QoS path is never
  exercised and the pass is false.

  a) with the access-group still bound:
     .65 -> 100% packet loss
  b) x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44979
     x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
       interface port1.0.48
                                            <-- NO access-group line
     .65 -> 0% packet loss                  <-- PROOF the ACL path is dead
  c) x960-56YSQ(config)#mls qos enable
       Traffic will stop while configuration is applied. Continue? (y/n): y
     x960-56YSQ(config)#class-map t44979cm ; match access-group t44979
     x960-56YSQ(config)#policy-map t44979pm ; class t44979cm
     x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; service-policy input t44979pm
     x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
       service-policy input t44979pm        <-- and still no access-group
     host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss     DENIED
     host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss     PERMITTED
  => STEP 6 PASS: the same named hardware ACL enforces through `service-policy input`
     as it does through `access-group`. Because the access-group was proven removed and
     the traffic proven flowing beforehand, the deny in (c) can only have come from the
     QoS path, and its control still passed.

STEP 7 -- persistence across a reload                                     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Pre-flight: /tftproot/x960-tb277.rel present, 57,496,435 bytes (the DUT netboots).
x960-56YSQ#show boot  ->  Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)
  Config under test: service-policy removed, `access-group t44979` restored.
host# ping .70 from .65 -> 100% packet loss             (denied before the reload)
x960-56YSQ#copy running-config startup-config -> Building configuration... [OK]
x960-56YSQ#reload -> 'reboot system? (y/n):' y ; login seen after 119 s

  AFTER THE RELOAD:
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44979   4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32   SURVIVED
  Hardware IP access list t44979b  4 deny tcp  10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32   SURVIVED
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
   access-group t44979                                                          SURVIVED
x960-56YSQ#show static-channel-group
  Member: port1.0.2, port1.0.16                                                 SURVIVED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss     STILL DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss     STILL PERMITTED
  => STEP 7 PASS: the named ACL definitions, the port binding, the aggregator membership
     and the enforcement itself all survived a save and reload. Persistence is of the
     behaviour, not only of the configuration text, and the control still passed.

STEP 8 -- removal                                                         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44979
x960-56YSQ(config)#no access-list hardware t44979
x960-56YSQ(config)#no access-list hardware t44979b
x960-56YSQ(config)#no policy-map t44979pm
x960-56YSQ(config)#no class-map t44979cm
x960-56YSQ(config)#no mls qos                (NOT `no mls qos enable`, which is rejected)
x960-56YSQ#show access-list    -> empty
x960-56YSQ#show class-map      -> empty
x960-56YSQ#show policy-map     -> empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48   -> no access-group, no service-policy
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  Loop Detection      2
  ACL                 0
  QoS                 0
  Total               2 / 1024 (0.20%)      <-- EXACTLY the STEP 0 baseline
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss       traffic restored
  => STEP 8 PASS: clean removal. The configuration returned to its STEP 0 state, the
     denied traffic flowed again, and the classifier returned to its exact baseline
     occupancy, so nothing was stranded in hardware.
